# Use Arrow Functions

Prefer arrow functions over `function` declarations and expressions in JavaScript/TypeScript.

## When writing new code

- Use arrow functions for function expressions, callbacks, and methods where `this` binding is not required.
- Use arrow functions for React event handlers and hook callbacks unless you need a named function for recursion or debugging.

## Examples

```javascript
// ❌ BAD – function keyword
function double(x) {
  return x * 2;
}
const handler = function (e) {
  console.log(e);
};

// ✅ GOOD – arrow functions
const double = (x) => x * 2;
const handler = (e) => {
  console.log(e);
};
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD – function in array methods
items.map(function (item) {
  return item.id;
});

// ✅ GOOD – arrow function
items.map((item) => item.id);
```

## Exceptions

- Use a regular `function` when you need its own `this` (e.g. object methods that use `this`, or when the function is used as a constructor).
- Use a regular `function` when you need a named function for recursion or clearer stack traces, if the team prefers it for that case.
